  "_style": "Always address the user with respectful “ଆପଣ” and polite imperatives (-ନ୍ତୁ: “ସେଭ୍ କରନ୍ତୁ”, “ପଠାନ୍ତୁ”); never “ତୁ”, and use “ତୁମେ” only inside words the user sends to the partner (reactions, pings, letter starters: “ତୁମକୁ ମନେ ପଡ଼ୁଛି”). The couple together is “ଆପଣ ଦୁହେଁ” (possessive “ଆପଣ ଦୁହିଁଙ୍କର”), and things the couple owns are “ଆମ” when the user speaks (“ଆମ ବଗିଚା”, “ଆମ ଗଛ”). The partner is always “ଆପଣଙ୍କ ସାଥୀ” (never ସ୍ୱାମୀ/ସ୍ତ୍ରୀ/ପ୍ରେମିକ/ପ୍ରେମିକା); Odia verbs do not mark gender, so keep sentences neutral and avoid gendered nouns or adjectives. A partner's name placeholder (%@) is never inflected or glued to a case suffix (no “%@ଙ୍କୁ”, “%@ର”): rephrase so the name stands alone (“%@ ଏକ ମଞ୍ଜି ପଠାଇଛନ୍ତି”, “ସାଥୀ: %@”), using honorific plural verb forms (-ଛନ୍ତି) for the partner. Quote marks: curly “ ” outside, ‘ ’ inside; ellipsis is the single character “…”. End sentences with the danda “।” (not a Latin period); ? and ! stay as is, with no space before ? ! : ; and one space after; keep “ · ” separators, \\n, emoji and all placeholders exactly. Numbers stay Western digits (1, 2, 3 — not ୧, ୨, ୩) because placeholders render them; dates follow the system formatter, write day-month order in prose. Odia has no capital letters: keep buttons and tabs short (one or two words, verb + କରନ୍ତୁ only when needed), titles as plain noun phrases; keep Coupley, Premium, CoupleSync and AI in Latin script. Use standard Unicode Odia with the nukta letters ଡ଼ ଢ଼ and ୱ (not ଵ), the hasanta ୍ for word-final consonants in loanwords (“ଚାଟ୍”, “ଥିମ୍”), and ZWNJ (U+200C) before a suffix after a hasanta when needed (“କୁଇଜ୍‌ଗୁଡ଼ିକ”). Plural categories are [\"one\",\"other\"]: the noun usually stays singular after a number (“%lld ଦିନ” for both), so “one” and “other” may share the same text. Tone: warm, light, kind modern Odia, never flowery or Sanskrit-heavy where an everyday word exists; common English tech words (ଆକାଉଣ୍ଟ, ସେଟିଂସ୍, ୱିଜେଟ୍) are fine.",
